From 04ccd18d8fc2ebbc1d16cc3870b061951c19cd51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 03:44:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 04/12] n64: RDP timing: VI-mode factors, per-transfer bus
 contention and span penalty from hardware

The RDP model's corrections to the stacked-primitive tables were fitted to two scenes.
Replaying the games' own display lists on an NTSC console (rdp_timing v9, v10, v12 and
v15: twelve lists from Donkey Kong 64 and Beetle Adventure Racing dumped from ares and
re-run from a test ROM, alone and beside controlled CPU and RSP traffic, under every VI
mode, and with classes of commands removed) separates the three effects that the fixed
factors lumped together:

- Placement: the tables need 15 clocks more per read-modify-write span of a real list,
  not 40 (the game lists are 0.84-0.88 of the console with no penalty and 0.95-1.07 with
  15; the removed-command variants show the per-class shares within 3% of the console).
- Other bus masters: instead of scaling every span by 13%/19%, each transfer of another
  master now delays the RDP's queued work by what it costs on the console while the RDP
  is drawing: a CPU data cache fill 11.7 clocks, an instruction cache fill 8.5, a
  write-back 5, SP DMA 1.22 per 8 bytes (64 B: 10, 512 B: 77, 4 KiB: 656). The CPU side
  of the same measurements (v10/v12): under a drawing RDP a data cache fill takes 5 cycles
  longer, an instruction fill 6, a write-back 11.
- The VI: the fixed 7% becomes the measured cost of the VI mode in use. The games' mode
  (anti-aliasing always on, divot, dedither: three framebuffer lines fetched per output
  line) slows every RDP transfer by 11.7% at 320x240 16-bit and 32% at 640 pixels; the
  "resample" mode costs 3% whatever the width, "replicate" 0.4%. The CPU's transfers also
  wait behind that fetch (6/6/2 cycles for fill/instruction fill/write-back), and the
  per-transfer RDP losses above grow by the same factor (x1.25 in the games' mode).

A dirty data cache miss on the console fills first and writes the evicted line back from
the write buffer afterwards (a dirty miss costs 66 cycles against 41 for a clean one):
the CPU is now charged 4 cycles for the write-back and the following line transfer waits
until the bus is free, instead of 40 cycles up front.

Donkey Kong 64's DKTV demo still completes; its frame deltas over the whole demo are the
closest to the console so far (score 22 against 69 before). Beetle Adventure Racing's crash
scene matches the console (68/74/66 VI per 30 frames against 70/76/69); the intro runs
2.0 VI per frame where the console runs 2.5: both the CPU and the RDP sit within a few
percent of the 2-VI boundary there, on both sides of it.

